{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 1,
  "simulation_id": "sim-01",
  "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B052",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B020",
    "B046"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration has changed from B020 to B052. Optical lattice clocks embody a clean physical mechanism: magic-wavelength confinement removes a central perturbation while retaining an ensemble of precisely controlled atoms. The resulting platform is mature, independently realized, and already permits demonstrated precision measurement and fundamental tests without relying on speculative downstream applications. Katori and Ye offer an unusually coherent allocation between the central concept and decisive realization. Compared with my original preference, this achievement has a sharper boundary between the core physical advance and its wider field, and a more defensible two-person attribution.",
    "case_against": "The strongest reservation is that the full development of optical frequency metrology and the highest-performance lattice clocks involved further important contributions. The citation should therefore remain confined to the invention and realization of the lattice-clock architecture, rather than imply credit for all subsequent optical-clock development or depend on a future redefinition of the second.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that the magic-wavelength architecture, independent realization, and complementary roles of Katori and Ye make B052 an unusually complete and clearly attributable instrumental achievement.",
        "response": "I agree, particularly with the distinction between demonstrated physical capability and forecasts of future use. In reassessing B020, I find that B052 better satisfies the demand for an exact, limited achievement and a credit allocation that does not omit an equally indispensable architect of the central framework."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson regards DMFT as a mature theoretical framework with enduring explanatory and experimental relevance, while recognizing the need to test whether three names adequately capture its development.",
        "response": "I retain B046 as a strong alternative. Its connection between a tractable theoretical construction and correlated materials is compelling. But the same concern about distributed formative contributions that I identified for fluctuation theorems also remains relevant for DMFT; B052 presently offers the more sharply delimited achievement and credit."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth emphasizes that optical lattice clocks are a lasting fundamental advance whose merit lies in controlled atomic quantum phases and transition energies, not merely improved timekeeping.",
        "response": "I agree. This formulation resolves my earlier hesitation about treating the case as primarily metrological: the essential contribution is a broadly reproducible physical method for suppressing systematic shifts and controlling many-atom quantum systems at exceptional precision."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival examination confirms that Katori and Ye alone adequately delimit the original lattice-clock achievement.",
      "Whether the citation should mention the magic-wavelength principle explicitly or retain the broader formulation of optical lattice clocks."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
